  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="sp-faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <div class="sp-faq-inner">
      <p class="sp-section-label">Questions</p>
      <h2 class="sp-section-h2" id="faq-h2">Brick Installation FAQ<br><em>Answered Honestly</em></h2>
      <div class="sp-faq-list" role="list">

        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-1">
            How much does brick installation cost in Chicago?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-1" role="region">
            <p>Brick installation in Chicagoland typically costs $15–$35 per square foot depending on brick type, mortar complexity, and site access. A small outdoor feature or wall repair runs $2,000–$6,000. A full home exterior can range from $20,000 to $75,000+. Free on-site estimates at 773-656-2107.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-2">
            How do I know what brick type I need?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-2" role="region">
            <p>Brick selection depends on your home's style, the location of the installation, and your climate exposure. AMS helps you select the right brick type and source it before any work begins — we'll walk you through options on the estimate visit.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-3">
            How long does brick installation take?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-3" role="region">
            <p>Small projects (retaining walls, outdoor features, additions) typically run 1–5 days. A full exterior can take 1–3 weeks. We provide a detailed timeline at the estimate stage.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-4">
            Can you match existing brick on my home?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-4" role="region">
            <p>Yes — brick matching is one of our specialties. We source bricks that match your existing in color, texture, and size, and we mortar-match to your existing joints. The goal is that you can't see where old ends and new begins.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-5">
            What's the difference between Type N, S, and M mortar?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-5" role="region">
            <p>Type N is correct for most above-grade exterior walls — medium strength, flexible enough for brick movement. Type S is for below-grade or high-moisture environments. Type M is for foundations and hardscape. Using the wrong type causes premature brick failure. AMS matches every mortar mix to your specific brick and location.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-6">
            Do you install brick for both residential and commercial projects?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-6" role="region">
            <p>Yes — AMS does both. For commercial brick installation, see our Commercial Brick &amp; Stone service page. For custom home builds, see Custom Home Masonry.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-7">
            Is AMS licensed and insured for brick installation?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-7" role="region">
            <p>Yes. Licensed in Illinois (License #MA-104-018887), $2.5M general liability, workers' compensation on all crew members. Proof of insurance available before work starts.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-8">
            Do you offer free estimates for brick installation?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-8" role="region">
            <p>Yes — all estimates are free, on-site, and in writing. Call 773-656-2107 or fill out our contact form to schedule.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
